  <h2>VAT &amp; duty: the boring framework</h2>
  <p>
    The UK charges <strong>VAT</strong> on most goods imported for personal use above applicable thresholds, collected via carrier or Royal Mail handling processes.
    Exact rates and administration change with HMRC policy—verify on <a href="https://www.gov.uk/goods-sent-from-abroad" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GOV.UK: goods sent from abroad</a> before you treat this article as tax advice.
    We are not accountants; we explain how spreadsheet buyers typically get surprised:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Undeclared optimism:</strong> declaring $10 on a branded parka invites holds—not savings.</li>
    <li><strong>Carrier fees:</strong> disbursement/handling fees stack on top of tax lines.</li>
    <li><strong>Multi-parcel hauls:</strong> splitting to “avoid VAT” can backfire if customs correlates address timing.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
